    "created_at": "2026-10-10T13:22:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-10T13:22:50Z",
    "body": "Adding numbers from the 3D side, since the canvas part was addressed in #87553 and #87558 but the scene shader still compiles the same way.\n\nTested on 4.7.2-stable, Pixel 5 (Adreno 620), Compatibility renderer, empty shader cache. The code below is unchanged on current master (232e6b1).\n\nOn the first bind of a material, [`_initialize_version()`](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/ed1daf0bf001b61586d9930840f2f1394092c079/drivers/gles3/shader_gles3.cpp#L703-L719) compiles all four [scene modes](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/ed1daf0bf001b61586d9930840f2f1394092c079/drivers/gles3/shaders/scene.glsl#L2-L7) with `specialization_default_mask`. In the scene shader that default has every light type enabled. The render path usually requests a different specialization (for example with omni and spot disabled), so [`_version_bind_shader()`](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/ed1daf0bf001b61586d9930840f2f1394092c079/drivers/gles3/shader_gles3.h#L183-L212) then compiles that one synchronously as well. An opaque mesh drawn once in `mode_color` ends up with five linked programs, and often only one of them is used.\n\nOn the Pixel 5, drawing each of our materials once over two loading frames took 15.6 s of compile time, leaving 26 `SceneShaderGLES3` entries in the cache. The frame with 9 lit materials took about 10 s (roughly 1.1 s per material), the frame with the other 25 took 5.3 s (about 0.2 s each). Without that warm-up, the setup step that first draws the level blocked the main thread for 8.0 s on a cold cache, against about 0.5 s with a warm cache. A 30 s perfetto trace of a cold start had 323 main-thread samples inside the Adreno compiler (`libllvm-glnext`), against 2 in a cold run where every material had been warmed up first. With a warm cache none of this shows up.\n\nI haven't measured how much of that goes to the unused default-specialization programs. My rough guess is that compiling only the requested mode and specialization would cut cold compile time by half or more for typical opaque materials, but that is an estimate.\n\nCompiling modes lazily on first use, as was done for canvas, looks like the obvious first step. I can put together an MRP if that helps.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
