    "created_at": "2026-11-01T23:33:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-06T05:51:14Z",
    "body": "Numbers from the web side, in case they help with #124566. 4.7.2-stable, Compatibility, Chrome on Windows (ANGLE, D3D11), fresh browser profile per run, timed by wrapping `linkProgram`/`getProgramParameter` in an init script and measuring to the first `LINK_STATUS` query.\n\nThe scene is a 3D level with about 40 distinct materials (mostly StandardMaterial3D, a few spatial shaders), depth fog, two-split directional shadows and glow.\n\n| | Programs | Compile + link | First frame, cold |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Stock 4.7.2 template | 183 | 63 to 103 s | 85 to 125 s |\n| Template with the default-specialization compile in `_initialize_version()` skipped | 74 | 10.5 s | 31 s |\n\n- 117 of the 154 scene programs in the stock run carried the default specialization (`APPLY_TONEMAPPING`, no fog, no PSSM, every light type enabled). They averaged about 0.8 s each against about 0.3 s for the fog/PSSM2 specializations the frames draw with.\n- Skipping them changed nothing I could see in screenshots, and the worst in-play hitch went from 4 to 5 s down to about 1 s.\n- On 4.7.2 the skip only took effect with `rendering/shader_compiler/shader_cache/enabled` off for web, because `shader_cache_dir_valid` is true there (user:// exists). Your `_is_shader_cache_enabled()` already returns false on web, so that part is covered.\n- Question: #124566 still compiles `specialization_default_mask` for every variant in `_initialize_version()`. Is the intent that the defaults now match what scene rendering requests? On web that loop was most of our load time. I haven't tested the PR build itself yet.\n\nSeparately, each GPUParticles3D process shader cost 22 to 25 s in ANGLE (details and a suspected cause in #87843), so I moved those to CPUParticles3D.\n\nIf it helps, I am happy to build and measure #124566 (or any other patch) on the web side; the timing setup is ready to rerun.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
